<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Ganga dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350–<a href="/years/1000">1000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Western Gangas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Ganga dynasty ruled southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 350 to 1000, with capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolar">Kolar</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talakad">Talakad</a>. Often subordinate allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> and then of the Rashtrakutas, they were major patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>; the colossal statue of Gommateshwara at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> was erected by their minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavundaraya">Chavundaraya</a>. Their kingdom fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> around 1000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty.jpg" alt="Western Ganga dynasty" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mlpkr · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western-ganga-empire-map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Western Gangas are called by that name to distinguish them from the Eastern Gangas, who later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>. Mythical accounts point to a northern origin, while theories based on inscriptions suggest a southern one. According to some records the family belonged to the Kanvayana gotra and traced its lineage to the Ikshvakus of the solar dynasty. It is theorised that the Gangas took advantage of the confusion caused by the invasion of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by the northern king Samudra Gupta before 350, and carved out a kingdom for themselves. The area they controlled was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangavadi">Gangavadi</a> and included parts of the modern districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, Hassan, Mandya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumkur">Tumkur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolar">Kolar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>.</p><p>The Western Gangas began their rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolar">Kolar</a> and later moved their capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talakad">Talakadu</a> on the Kaveri River. After the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> they accepted Chalukya overlordship and fought on their behalf against the Pallavas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a>. In 753 the Rashtrakutas replaced the Chalukyas as the dominant power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and after a century of struggle for autonomy the Gangas accepted Rashtrakuta overlordship and fought alongside them against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>. In the late tenth century the Rashtrakutas were replaced north of the Tungabhadra by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a>, and the Cholas regained power south of the Kaveri. The defeat of the Western Gangas by the Cholas around 1000 ended Ganga influence in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Ganga administration drew on principles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a>. The kingdom was divided into Rashtra, or districts, and these into Visaya and Desa, and from the eighth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> word Nadu replaced Visaya. Inscriptions name officials such as the prime minister, the treasurer and the foreign minister, all carrying the additional title of commander. Local landlords called gavundas collected taxes, kept records of landownership and raised militia when required. The Gangas patronised all the major religions of the time, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, Vedic Brahmanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>. Jainism became popular within the dynasty in the eighth century, when King Shivamara I built many Jain basadis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Literature in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> flourished under the dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavundaraya">Chavundaraya</a>'s Chavundaraya Purana of 978 is an early surviving work of Kannada prose. The monolith of Gomateshwara at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a>, commissioned by Chavundaraya, is carved from fine-grained white granite and stands about 18 metres tall. The Gangas used Kannada and Sanskrit in administration, and an inscription of 890 found at Beguru near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> is the earliest mention of the name of Bangalore.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western Ganga dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The core territory around Vatapi was ruled directly through provinces and districts known as vishaya, but much of the kingdom was held through subordinate dynasties such as the <b>Western Ganga dynasty</b>, the Alupa dynasty and the Sendrakas, who paid tribute and supplied troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Dantidurga died c. 756 without a son and was succeeded by his uncle Krishna I, who completed the conquest of the remaining Chalukya lands, subdued the <b>Western Ganga dynasty</b> of southern Karnataka and attacked the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He took advantage of the sack of the Rashtrakuta capital Manyakheta by the Paramara Siyaka II in 972, overthrew the last Rashtrakuta king, Karka II, and then defeated the loyalist resistance led by the <b>Western Ganga</b> ruler Marasimha II, whose candidate Indra IV died in 982.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south, Jainism flourished in Karnataka and Tamil Nadu in the first millennium CE under dynasties such as the <b>Western Gangas</b>, whose minister Chavundaraya had the great statue of Bahubali carved at Shravanabelagola around 983; Jain monks contributed greatly to early literature in Tamil and Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
